  "query_preview": "into Wednesday afternoon. However that will be heavily \ndependent on how convection from Tuesday evening through \nWednesday morning evolves and if any new convection develops \nWednesday afternoon. Right now, the most likely area that may \nneed to be extend would be along and south of the Missouri \nRiver. After collaborating with our neighbors, decided to hold \noff given the uncertainty with the convection and potential \neffects on temperatures. If I had to put a probability to \nwhether the areas I mentioned are extended, I'd put it around \n50-60%. So there is a fair amount of uncertainty as a result of \nthe front and potential for new convection along it. \n\nSpeaking of fronts, a fairly robust front will be moving into the \nregion late Tuesday afternoon and evening and slowly move south\nthrough the overnight hours. Robust CAPE will build through the\nday ahead of the front across northern MO/ northeastern KS. \nShear will be limited and decrease with southward extent as \nstronger flow aloft remains well north of the area. But the \nfront will provide a focus for convective development, most \nlikely in the 00Z to 03Z time frame in our far northern/ \nnorthwestern zones. Initial development will pose a risk for \ndownburst winds and large hail. But this should trend to mainly \na wind hazard with time as front slides southward through the \novernight hours. Then for Wednesday afternoon, the forecast is \ndependent on where that front or any outflow boundaries reside. \nWith the latest suite of guidance, the most probable area for \nrenewed convection looks to be in our southern zones. CAPE may \npotentially be robust with ensemble mean SBCAPE values in excess\nof 1500 J/kg. But this could be as high 2500 J/kg based on the \n90th percentile of ensemble guidance. Shear may also be stronger\nthan Tuesday as the mid/upper level jet has shifted southward. \n\nThat cold front will finally bring an end to the extreme heat that \nhas plagued the area for nearly two weeks. High temperatures by \nThursday should be closer to seasonal normals, which now are in the \nlower 80s to near 80 by the end of the week. Dewpoints are also \nexpected to fall into the middle 60s for most Thursday afternoon. \nTemperatures rebound some for the weekend but we should still see \nhighs in the middle 80s through the end of the forecast.",
